#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# lerch_phi

#% text
# `lerch_phi(z, s, a)` gives the Lerch transcendent, the sum of `z^k/(k + a)^s` for `k >= 0`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The series (DLMF 25.14.1) converges for `abs(z) < 1`; at `z = 1` with `s > 1` it is the Hurwitz zeta function.

#% item
# Special cases: `lerch_phi(z, s, 1)` is `polylog(s, z)/z`.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
lerch_phi(0.5, 2, 1)
#% output
#= 1.164481052930025

#% cell
lerch_phi(0.5, 2.0, 0.5)
#% output
#= 4.27714550058095

#% cell
lerch_phi(-0.5, 1.0, 1.0)
#% output
#= 0.8109302162163288

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The polylogarithm case:

#% cell
lerch_phi(0.3, 3, 1) - polylog(3, 0.3)/0.3
#% output
#= 0.0

#% text
# The series:

#% cell
lerch_phi(0.4, 2.0, 0.7) - sum(0.4^k/(k + 0.7)^2, [k, 0, 80])
#% output
#= 0.0

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# On the unit circle, where the alternating series still converges for `z = -1`, the value is not computed:

#% cell
lerch_phi(-1.0, 1.0, 1.0)
#% output
#= lerch_phi(-1.0, 1.0, 1.0)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `polylog`, `zeta`
